![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

Comparing URLs as raw strings is brittle and can lead to infinite redirect loops. Browsers often normalize URLs (e.g., adding trailing slashes), so `https://example.com` might not match `https://example.com/`. Using the `URL` constructor for comparison ensures that both URLs are normalized before checking for equality.

```suggestion
  try {
    if (new URL(currentUrl).href === new URL(blockUrl).href) return;
  } catch (e) {
    if (currentUrl === blockUrl) return;
  }
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The `settingsReady` promise lacks error handling. If `chrome.storage.sync.get` fails or rejects, this promise will remain in a rejected state, causing any navigation listener that awaits it to crash and potentially allow blocked navigations (fail-open). Adding a `.catch()` ensures the service worker remains functional even if storage access fails.

```javascript
const settingsReady = chrome.storage.sync.get({ settings: DEFAULT_SETTINGS })
  .then(({ settings }) => {
    currentSettings = normalizeSettings(settings);
  })
  .catch(err => console.error('[journal-blocker] Failed to load settings:', err));
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The `chrome.tabs.update` call is an asynchronous operation that can fail (e.g., if the tab is closed or the URL is invalid). Adding a `.catch()` block prevents unhandled promise rejections and allows for better error logging.

```javascript
  chrome.tabs.update(tabId, { url: blockUrl }).catch(err => {
    console.warn('[journal-blocker] Redirect failed:', err);
  });
```

